keep in mind i did not test this, going off the documentation here.

Closing the comment since it should be easy to refactor cases like that.

integer division in floating point context can be intended.

The example
```
// Warn, floating-point values expected.
double d;
int i = 42;
d = 32 * 8 / (2 + i);
```

The suggested fix is to add a cast but that would make the expression less readable.

Example of a coroutine taking reference parameters:

https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-bmc-code-mgmt/blob/de331ae49afd6fe27271c4081ffa239746bda8b5/bios/bios_software_manager.cpp#L22

```
sdbusplus::async::task<bool> BIOSSoftwareManager::initDevice(
    const std::string& service, const std::string& path, SoftwareConfig& config)
{
    std::string configIface =
        "xyz.openbmc_project.Configuration." + config.configType;

    std::optional<uint64_t> spiControllerIndex =
        co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty<uint64_t>(
            ctx, service, path, configIface, "SPIControllerIndex");

    if (!spiControllerIndex.has_value())
    {
        error("Missing property: SPIControllerIndex");
        co_return false;
    }

    std::optional<uint64_t> spiDeviceIndex =
        co_await dbusGetRequiredProperty<uint64_t>(
            ctx, service, path, configIface, "SPIDeviceIndex");
```

In that case the `const std::string& service` reference is used across a `co_await` and could theoretically be dangling at that point.

But it's working code. If you intend to rework all cases like that to `shared_ptr` then i guess we can keep the check.

But `shared_ptr` has it's own problems.
